arguments.content:
⚠️ **IMPORTANT: The resolver-less pass-through path has no test**

The PR's third leg — "read failures degrade to a structured message, never an uncaught tool error" — is only tested *with* a resolver wired (`task_observe_live_transcript_test.dart` "unreadable transcript degrades to a structured error"). The branch where a **placeholder handle meets a real reader but NO resolver** (`isPlaceholder && resolveSessionPath != null` is false, so `readMessages` is called on the `<parentSessionId>/<id>` placeholder directly) is untested — and it is exactly the path embedded hosts (no sessions-tree scan) ride, and exactly the regression class this ticket fixes: pre-PR it was the uncaught `SessionException`.

Suggested additions to `test/task/task_observe_live_transcript_test.dart`:

```dart
test('placeholder handle WITHOUT a resolver degrades to a structured '
    'error, never a throw', () async {
  await mgr.register(id: 'a1', name: 'worker', agentType: 'task', task: 'x');
  await mgr.update('a1', status: SubagentStatus.running);
  final tools = subagentMonitoringTools(
    manager: mgr,
    readMessages: jsonlChildMessageReader(env), // no resolveSessionPath
  );
  final tool = tools.firstWhere((t) => t.name == 'task_observe');
  final result = await tool.execute({'id': 'a1'}, null, null);
  final text = result.content.whereType<TextContent>().map((b) => b.text).join();
  expect(text, contains('transcript unavailable'));
  expect(text, contains('running'));
});
```

The `sessionPath.isEmpty` placeholder branch (line 1103) deserves the same one-line guard. The behavior is implemented correctly (the `try`/`catch` below wraps every read) — this is purely a missing regression guard.

💡 **SUGGESTION: "try again shortly" is misleading for terminal children**

This branch answers "no transcript available yet … the child session file appears at its first turn boundary; try again shortly" for **every** resolver-null observe of a placeholder handle — including children that will *never* flush: a `failed` child that died before its first boundary, a spawn that never reached the loop, or an executor without a `childSessionFactory`. The parent is told to retry forever on a transcript that will never exist.

The status is correctly carried (`status: ${handle.status.name}`), so this is wording polish, but a status-conditioned hint would save the parent a doomed retry loop:

```dart
final hint = handle.status == SubagentStatus.running
    ? 'the child session file appears at its first turn boundary; '
        'try again shortly'
    : 'the child never produced a transcript';
return ToolExecutionResult.text(
  'no transcript available yet for "$id" (status: '
  '${handle.status.name}) — $hint',
);
```

💡 **SUGGESTION: Resolution failure is swallowed and misdirects the read error**

```dart
try {
  resolved = await resolveSessionPath(handle.id);
} on Object {
  // Resolution is best-effort — the read below reports honestly.
}
```

When the scan throws, `sessionPath` stays the `<parentSessionId>/<id>` placeholder and the subsequent read fails on that synthetic path — the structured message then quotes an error like `Failed to read session 'parent-1/a1': No such file or directory`, sending whoever debugs it hunting for a file that was never a real path. The degradation itself is right (best-effort resolution is the correct contract); consider surfacing that the *resolution* leg failed so the follow-up error is attributable:

```dart
} on Object catch (resolveError) {
  // Resolution is best-effort — the read below reports honestly, but
  // name the resolution failure so the error is attributable.
  resolutionError = resolveError;
}
```

…then append `; path resolution failed: $resolutionError` to the "transcript unavailable" text when set. Low priority — `repo.list` only throws if the sessions tree itself is unreadable, in which case the read fails too.
